  "Number": 7,
  "Question": "For which nonzero real numbers \\(x\\) is \\(\\frac{|x-|x||}{x}\\) a positive integer?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "for negative \\(x\\) only",
    "B": "for positive \\(x\\) only",
    "C": "only for \\(x\\) an even integer",
    "D": "for all nonzero real numbers \\(x\\)",
    "E": "for no nonzero real numbers \\(x\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "The value of \\(|x|\\) depends on the sign of \\(x\\), so consider the two possible signs. The condition \\(x\\ne0\\) ensures that the denominator is nonzero.\n\nIf \\(x>0\\), then \\(|x|=x\\), giving\n\\[\n\\frac{|x-|x||}{x}=\\frac{|x-x|}{x}=0.\n\\]\nIf \\(x<0\\), then \\(|x|=-x\\). Thus \\(x-|x|=2x<0\\), whose absolute value is \\(-2x\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\frac{|x-|x||}{x}=\\frac{-2x}{x}=-2.\n\\]\nThese cases exhaust all nonzero real numbers. Neither \\(0\\) nor \\(-2\\) is a positive integer, so there are no qualifying values of \\(x\\). The answer is choice E.",
